Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
hey everyone i'm tamor and i recently  got to see eldon ring in action yep you  heard me right i got to see 15 minutes  of the game as well as get a  presentation from from software's  yasuhiro kital and i've thrown together  this quick collection of seven brand new  things that i learned about the game for  more on elder ring make sure to like  this video and subscribe to gamespot  [Music]  yep you read that right from software  games are known for their intricately  connected worlds where narrative is  delivered in fragmented pieces leaving  the player to make connections filling  gaps and draw conclusions so when rumors  that elden ring would be an open world  title started spill out people naturally  had questions but according to from  software's yasira kital however the team  didn't set out to even make an open  world title in fact from software isn't  even sure it has the correct definition  of what encompasses an open world  experience and so it isn't quick to call  elden ring one to be clear it is  unmistakably an open world game but one  that's cut from the same cloth as breath  of the wild rather than any of the  recent assassin's creed titles the  distinguishing factor between them for  me is how deliberate the construction of  the world feels and what the  moment-to-moment experience of  adventuring is like the lands between  where elder ring takes place is an  interconnected world that while vast  didn't look like it was big just for the  sake of being big visually it's  incredibly dense whether standing atop  craggy cliffs peering out over a land  blanketed in an ominous fog or trudging  through flooded cities from software  wants you to always be drawn towards  something of interest on the horizon and  hopes that along the way you may  accidentally stumble into something  unexpected like breath of the wild the  developer is counting on curiosity to be  your guide instead of dozens of icons on  a map similarly the things that you  stumble upon or have thrust in front of  you feel more emergent than in most open  world games in the hands of demo i saw  one such moment was a dragon suddenly  swooping down and engaging the player in  any other souls born game the telltale  signs of a scripted boss fight would  appear the terrifying but epic  orchestral score accompanied by a life  bar and a fog gate that signifies you  are definitely trapped but these  elements did not appear it was just an  incredibly pissed off dragon looking to  pick off a wandering warrior scattered  throughout the overworld are dungeons  that can be stumbled upon while  exploring these in classic from software  fashion are sometimes deadly detours  that invite the player to overcome traps  enemies hiding in dark corners and the  occasional boss in order to find  treasures interestingly the one eyesore  felt reminiscent of a bloodborne chalice  dungeon depending on your opinion of the  divisive chalice dungeons that may not  sound particularly enticing but i didn't  see enough of these dungeons to say  whether each of them is bespoke in its  design or procedurally generated like  many of the blood-borne chalice dungeons  however everything you know and love  from the soulsborne dungeon is otherwise  present there's intimidatingly large  blades swinging around environments  ready to cleave you into at the  slightest misstep there's monsters that  pounce on you as soon as you emerge from  a doorway and there's even a wooden  chest with a chain curved upwards  also scattered around the world are what  from software calls legacy dungeons the  name is intended to signify that these  areas present something akin to the  traditional souls-born gameplay  experience in a contained environment  the legacy dungeon we ventured into was  stormvale castle which in classic  soulsborne fashion is an expansive  medieval structure that dares the player  to breach its gates however a weathered  beastly looking figure wearing an ornate  book as a necklace who can be found  skulking in a dark corner nearby advises  you to do otherwise stormville's gates  are closed but he can request they be  open for you a foul tarnished in search  of the elden ring here's where things  change in a more obvious fashion the  player is presented with a choice they  can either request that the strange  figure give the signal to open the gate  or heed the warning and take a back  route that is much safer bold  adventurers will want to go through the  front gate but given the immediate  barrage of arrows that followed the  moment the tarnish crossed the threshold  i suspect they will realize that the  back route is the smarter option picking  one doesn't lock you out of the other  path however instead this choice is  presented as a kind of on-the-fly  difficulty decision difficult challenges  are the cornerstone of from software's  games but according to kital from  software's intention is never to create  a difficult game just for the sake of it  instead the team wants players to enjoy  the experience clear the hurdles and see  the game through it creates games with a  sense of value derived from overcoming  hardships and elden ring implements new  ways to do this one of these is by  taking mechanics from its last game  sekuro like zekiro's protagonist wolf  the tarnished is agile capable of  leaping into the air at a moment's noise  in gameplay terms this means that  platforming is now more integral to the  gameplay experience and as such  stormville castle is designed with  verticality in mind the tarnish is shown  jumping from rooftop to rooftop slinking  through an open window and down into the  castle out in the fields of the lands  between the tarnish can engage in  stealth avoiding combat entirely or  adopting an assassination oriented  playstyle in outright battle meanwhile a  leaping heavy attack will break through  an enemy starts leaving them vulnerable  in addition to creating exciting new  gameplay opportunities within the  soulsborne framework this new injection  of agility means people who may not be  as confident in standing their ground  and fighting towering beasts imposing  knights or deadly magicians have a way  to take some semblance of control in  battle they can dictate their pace a  little more another way they can turn  the tides in their favor is by summoning  what are essentially minions that will  fight for you these function similarly  to co-op partners but with decidedly  less staying power the summons i saw  didn't seem like they could stand up to  a boss but against a mob of enemies they  were effective at taking the pressure  off and even thinning out the crowd  kital said that elden ring aims to make  the most of its sprawling world so there  will be plenty of interesting places and  plenty of interesting people to discover  too from software describes the story of  elden ring as a multi-protagonist drama  while facing old heroes and demigods in  search of the elden ring the tarnish  will encounter a variety of characters  and the game is more focused on them  than any of from software's previous  titles characters have always been  crucial to the narrative of the  soulsborne titles you only have to look  at the fandom surrounding solaire sigma  mikolaish or genichiro and many of the  others to know how important they are to  from software's games and the people  that play them elder ring aims to double  down on characters to address the fact  that for many from software's games have  stories that are difficult to pass by  focusing on characters and the drama  surrounding them the narrative is  painted in a much clearer way  establishing a strong baseline that  anyone can enjoy that's not to say it  will be as straightforward as held as  something like sakura george r r  martin's involvement all but guarantees  that the song of ice and fire also laid  the foundation for elden rings mythos  creating characters rivalries political  entry corrupted lineages and more and  from software has built on this  foundation with its own brand of  storytelling according to qatar not all  of martin's work is represented in the  final game in some cases characters he  created aren't in the game but their  place within the history of the world is  represented this is the kind of thing  that will no doubt serve as fodder for  the obsessive soulsborne community to  dig into  that's a few things that we learned  about eldon ring for my full preview  head over to gamespot.com and for more  elden ring make sure to like this video  and subscribe to gamespot you can find  me on twitter at tomorrow h  you

